content.text:
All 20 tests pass in ~5.7s and properly mock the external dependencies. Here's my review:

```json
{
  "approved": true,
  "feedback": "The tests are well-structured, comprehensive, and address the major concerns from the review feedback. They properly mock `@harms-haus/engin` (using `mock.module`) so no real AI calls are made — this is confirmed by the ~300ms per-test runtime (driven entirely by the 6×50ms stub-phase delays in the workflow, not by network calls). The mocks for `resolveProfilesDirs` and `loadProfilesFromDirs` eliminate any dependency on global profile configuration at `~/.config/engin/`. Test coverage is thorough: lifecycle ordering, title generation, multiple error fallback paths, partial/missing onStatus, phase structure, indicator progression, agent spawn/complete callbacks, argument passthrough, harness disposal, and duration reporting. A few minor improvements are noted below but none block approval.",
  "issues": [
    {
      "file": "tests/workflows/develop.test.ts",
      "description": "The 50ms `setTimeout` delays in each stub phase (6 phases × 50ms = 300ms) make every full-workflow test take ~300ms. With 18 such tests, this accounts for ~5.4s of the 5.73s total runtime. Consider reducing the delay to a smaller value (e.g., 10ms or 0ms) or making it configurable/token-based so the workflow is test-friendly while still demonstrating async behavior. The delay serves a purpose in production but is unnecessarily slow for tests.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "file": ".engin/workflows/develop/main.ts",
      "description": "Resource leak: when `session.prompt` rejects inside the `try` block, the catch handler sets the fallback title but never calls `dispose()`. The harness session is left open. The test 'uses fallback title when session.prompt rejects' should verify that `dispose` is either called (preferred) or explicitly note that it's leaked. Consider wrapping the agent operations in a try/finally to guarantee disposal on all exit paths.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/workflows/develop.test.ts",
      "description": "The `afterAll` restore via `mock.module('@harms-haus/engin', () => realEngin)` uses a static snapshot captured at import time via `Object.assign({}, await import(...))`. This may miss non-enumerable properties or live bindings on the real module namespace. Since Bun isolates test files per process, this is unlikely to cause cross-file pollution in practice, but a more robust approach would be to not capture the real module at all and instead define the mock with only the exports needed by the workflow (using `satisfies` types to catch missing exports at compile time).",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/workflows/develop.test.ts",
      "description": "The test 'uses fallback title when createHarness rejects' calls `onAgentSpawn` before `await harness` rejects, so the spawn notification fires but no corresponding `onAgentComplete` fires. The test doesn't assert on this asymmetric behavior. If the workflow is expected to not send spawn on failure, the `onAgentSpawn` call should be moved after the `await`. If it's acceptable, add an assertion confirming `onAgentSpawn` was called with the expected args.",
      "severity": "minor"
    }
  ]
}
```
